+        /* RADIANCE: AN ARGUMENT THE SCHEMA DOES NOT DECLARE MUST NOT TAKE THE REPLY DOWN WITH IT.
+         *
+         * Every arm above matches its parameter name as a LITERAL, so a name outside the schema
+         * matches nothing, the call matches nothing, and p.end() makes that the whole reply --
+         * which comes back to the caller as prose with the XML in it. Observed on MiniCPM5-2B
+         * calling a `read` tool whose only parameter is `path`:
+         *
+         *     <function name="read"><param name="action">read</param></function>
+         *
+         * one hallucinated argument name, and the turn was lost. The model then reads its own
+         * call echoed back as text, concludes the tool is broken, and rewrites it.
+         *
+         * Tried LAST, so every declared name still binds to its own schema-checked arm and
+         * nothing that parses today parses differently. The value is taken the way a string
+         * argument takes it -- the text up to the value suffix, which is what delimits it in
+         * this format. What is given up is knowing that the argument was undeclared; the TOOL
+         * gets to say so, which it can do properly, and which a reply that never arrives cannot.
+         * Same argument as the non-JSON container fallback above. */
+        /* WHAT ENDS THE NAME IS NOT ALWAYS `name_suffix`. This format decomposes
+         * `<param name="path">v</param>` as prefix `<param name="`, name_suffix EMPTY and
+         * value_prefix `">` -- so a generic name runs until value_prefix here, and the first
+         * cut of this arm guarded on `!name_suffix.empty()` and silently never built itself. */
+        const std::string & name_end =
+            arguments.name_suffix.empty() ? arguments.value_prefix : arguments.name_suffix;
+        if (!arguments.name_prefix.empty() && !name_end.empty() &&
+            !arguments.value_suffix.empty()) {
+            auto unknown_arg =
+                p.tool_arg(p.tool_arg_open(arguments.name_prefix +
+                                           p.tool_arg_name(p.until(name_end)) +
+                                           arguments.name_suffix) +
+                           arguments.value_prefix + p.tool_arg_string_value(until_suffix) +
+                           p.tool_arg_close(p.literal(arguments.value_suffix)));
+            any_arg |= p.rule("tool-" + name + "-arg-undeclared", unknown_arg);
+        }
+
